      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "It has been shown that leukocytes play one of the key roles in the myocardial reperfusion injury."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To examine the effects of cardiac protection with leukocyte-depleted blood cardioplegia on the early outcome of patients with preserved left ventricular function who undergo surgical revascularisation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study group consisted of 58 patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) who were randomised to receive leukocyte-depleted blood cardioplegia (leukocyte filter Pall BC1B) (group A, n=29) or to receive standard blood cardioplegia (group B, n=29). Peri-operative mortality and morbidity as well as haemodynamical and biochemical parameters were compared between these two groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No early death occurred. There were no statistical differences in clinical data between the groups. Only cardiac index measured 24 hours after declamping of aorta was significantly higher in group A than in group B (3.6+/-0.6 l/min/m(2) vs 2.95+/-0.45 l/min/m(2), p<0.05). Group B showed significant higher release of creatine kinase (CK) 6 and 12 hours, and CK-MB 6, 12, and 24 hours after unclumping the aorta whereas troponin I level was similar in both groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The use of leukocyte-depleted blood cardioplegia during elective CABG did not improve the early outcome."
        }
      ]},
